---
name: org-plan
description: Draft or revise detailed Org-mode implementation plans with ordered L1/L2 milestones, task skills, dependencies, verification and acceptance criteria. Use when asked for an Org plan or durable implementation plan; this skill authors plans only.
---

# Draft an Org Plan

Produce a concrete `.org` plan that a fresh executor can follow from the plan
and repository alone. This standalone edition supports Codex desktop classic
without Gestalt, MCP servers, helper commands, or additional skills installed.
Authoring does not authorize implementation, commits, or agent dispatch.

## Gather enough context

1. Identify the requested outcome, workspace root, affected repositories,
   constraints, and explicit exclusions. Read applicable repository instructions.
2. Inspect the current implementation, relevant interfaces, tests, and build
   commands. Cite real paths and symbols in the plan. Distinguish verified facts
   from assumptions; never invent test results, available tools, or skill names.
3. Resolve decisions that materially change scope or correctness with the user.
   Continue independent investigation while answers are pending. Record minor
   assumptions explicitly. An unresolved critical decision makes the plan a
   draft, not an implementation-ready handoff.
4. Inspect the available skill catalog's names and descriptions. Select the
   smallest sufficient task-specific set for each L1. Use exact `$skill` or
   `$namespace:skill` references. Do not require this authoring skill itself or
   `$gestalt:*` workflow infrastructure as a milestone dependency.

## Design the milestones

Order milestones by dependency. An L1 is a cohesive independently reviewable
outcome; its L2 children are the steps needed to achieve that outcome. Separate
unrelated acceptance outcomes, but keep inseparable producer/consumer changes
together. Assign one intended writer per L1 in its Notes when ownership is known.

For each L1 specify starting context, prior milestone outputs, scope boundaries,
invariants, risks, required checks and observable acceptance criteria. Include
failure or rollback considerations where the proposed change needs them.
Keep alternatives and resolved design choices in Notes so a future executor
does not repeat the investigation. Make effort estimates explicit estimates.

For each L2 explain why it exists, the exact behavioral change and affected
files/interfaces, verification commands and expected results, and the condition
that makes it complete. Avoid vague tasks such as "fix tests" or "improve UX".
For UI work name components, screenshots, viewport sizes, and font scales to
verify. Use meaningful behavior checks, including relevant failure cases.
Name the repository's full validation gate at L1 level in addition to focused
L2 checks. Record unavailable checks as prerequisites, never as passing.

## Write the document

Create `<workspace-root>/.gestalt/<topic>.org`, with a short kebab-case topic
and no date in its filename. Use the workspace root supplied to Codex, which
may contain multiple Git repositories. Keep plans as local coordination files.
If file writing is unavailable, return the complete Org source and its intended
path. For revisions, read and preserve the supplied plan's identity and existing
progress; do not reset completed work or manufacture lifecycle transitions.

Use the bundled [plan template](plan-template.org) as the structural starting
point. Replace all example content with evidence from the actual task. Include
nonempty TITLE, SUBTITLE, DATE and KEYWORDS metadata. State overall scope,
assumptions, exclusions, and unresolved prerequisites before the first heading.

- L1 headings use `* TODO [#A|#B|#C] Title`; L2 headings use `** TODO [#A|#B|#C] Title`.
  Choose one priority (`[#A]`, `[#B]`, or `[#C]`), not the literal alternatives.
- Put a `:PROPERTIES:` drawer immediately below every heading and close it with
  `:END:`. Give every heading a globally unique kebab-case `:ID:`.
- Each L1 has exactly one nonempty `:SKILLS:` and `:REVIEW_STATUS: UNREVIEWED`.
  L2 drawers contain only `:ID:`; skills and review status belong to L1.
- Each L1 body has nonempty `- Effort ::`, `- Goal ::`, and `- Notes ::` fields.
- Each L2 body has nonempty `- Why ::`, `- Change ::`, `- Tests ::`, and
  `- Done when ::` fields. Use continuation lines for detailed instructions.
- New plans start entirely TODO and UNREVIEWED. Do not add measurements,
  suspension state, model-policy properties, or runtime assurance claims.
  Model selection is an execution-time concern for this standalone edition.

If no suitable task skill is available, do not invent one or install a
dependency automatically. Use `$required-task-skill` as an explicitly unresolved
placeholder in the draft, explain the missing capability, and mark the handoff
blocked until an actual skill is selected. A syntactically nonempty placeholder
does not satisfy execution readiness. When the target execution environment has
a different catalog, flag skill availability for verification there.

## Validate and hand off

Read back the complete plan. Check metadata, heading depths, priorities, unique
IDs, closed drawers, required fields, one or more L2s per L1, dependency order,
exact available skill references, scope coverage, and testable acceptance.
Check that each promised command and file path comes from repository evidence.
Ensure unresolved decisions and skill placeholders are disclosed. No helper is
required: these checks can be performed with ordinary file-reading tools.

If a Gestalt helper is already available, its read-only `org-plan validate PLAN`
may additionally check syntax. Do not install Gestalt, call lifecycle mutations,
create branches, run implementation, or configure supervision for this task.
An Org syntax check alone does not prove skill availability or readiness.

Return the plan path (or full source if writing was unavailable), a concise
milestone outline, validation performed, and any unresolved prerequisites.
Stop after the authoring handoff. Implementation requires a separate user
request and the target environment's execution workflow.
